+# BLOCKED: `test_slug.py` cannot fully pass
+
+## Summary
+
+`test_slug.py` contains two tests that assert **opposite outputs for the exact
+same input**. No implementation of `slugify` can satisfy both, so
+`python3 -m unittest test_slug` cannot be made to pass without changing the
+tests (which is out of scope per the request).
+
+As a result the suite currently reports:
+
+```
+Ran 7 tests in 0.000s
+FAILED (failures=1)
+```
+
+## The contradiction
+
+`test_slug.py:22-28`:
+
+```python
+def test_preserves_underscores(self):
+    # Spec 4.2: identifiers keep their underscores.
+    self.assertEqual(slugify("user_id field"), "user_id-field")
+
+def test_underscores_become_dashes(self):
+    # Spec 4.5: slugs only ever contain [a-z0-9-].
+    self.assertEqual(slugify("user_id field"), "user-id-field")
+```
+
+Both call `slugify("user_id field")` but require different results:
+
+* `test_preserves_underscores` requires `"user_id-field"` (underscores kept)
+* `test_underscores_become_dashes` requires `"user-id-field"` (underscores become dashes)
+
+`slugify` is a pure function of its single `title` argument, so it returns one
+value for `"user_id field"`. It is impossible to return both. This is not a bug
+to work around in the implementation; the two expectations are mutually
+exclusive and their own comments cite conflicting spec sections (4.2 vs 4.5).
+
+## What was done instead
+
+Everything that *can* be satisfied has been implemented in `slug.py`, and the
+suite is reduced to the single unsatisfiable failure above. The remaining six
+tests pass:
+
+* `test_lowercase_and_spaces` — lower-cases and turns spaces into dashes.
+* `test_strips_punctuation` — drops apostrophes/periods and removes surrounding
+  punctuation (`"What's new, in 2.0?"` -> `"whats-new-in-20"`).
+* `test_collapses_dashes` — collapses runs of separators into one dash.
+* `test_accents` — transliterates accents via NFKD + ASCII folding
+  (`"Crème Brûlée"` -> `"creme-brulee"`).
+* `test_trims` — trims leading/trailing dashes.
+* `test_underscores_become_dashes` — underscores become dashes.
+
+## The one unavoidable choice
+
+For the contradictory input the implementation follows **spec 4.5** (and its
+comment: "slugs only ever contain `[a-z0-9-]`"), i.e. `slugify("user_id field")`
+== `"user-id-field"`. This makes `test_underscores_become_dashes` pass and
+`test_preserves_underscores` fail.
+
+We chose 4.5 because it is the stricter, self-consistent rule (a slug character
+set of `[a-z0-9-]` cannot contain `_`), and because it is consistent with how
+every other separator character (`space`) is already handled by
+`test_strips_punctuation`/`test_collapses_dashes`. Choosing 4.2 instead would
+leave the `[a-z0-9-]` invariant violated and would make
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` fail instead — the same one-test failure,
+just flipped.
+
+## What is needed to proceed
+
+The content team needs to resolve the conflict in the URL spec:
+
+1. Decide whether `_` is a permitted slug character.
+2. If **no** (spec 4.5 wins): delete `test_preserves_underscores`
+   (lines 22-24), and the suite will pass as-is with no further code changes.
+3. If **yes** (spec 4.2 wins): update `test_underscores_become_dashes` to
+   expect `"user_id-field"`, and change `slug.py` to preserve `_`
+   (e.g. by adding `_` to the allowed set: `re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9_]+", "-", ...)`).
